%S A400871 1,1,1,2,1,1,1,4,2,1,1,2,1,1,1,3,1,2,1,2,1,1,1,4,2,1,4,2,1,1,1,2,1,1,
%T A400871 1,4,1,1,1,4,1,1,1,2,2,1,1,3,2,2,1,2,1,4,1,4,1,1,1,2,1,1,2,10,1,1,1,2,
%U A400871 1,1,1,8,1,1,2,2,1,1,1,3,3,1,1,2,1,1,1,4
%N A400871 The number of ordered pairs of exponential divisors (d_1, d_2) of n^2 such d_1*d_2 is also an exponential divisor of n^2.
%C A400871 For nonsquare values the number of these pairs is 0.
%H A400871 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A400871/b400871.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400871 Multiplicative with a(p^e) = A385429(e).
%F A400871 Asymptotic mean: Limit_{m->oo} (1/m) * Sum_{k=1..m} a(k) = Product_{p prime} (1-1/p)*(1 + Sum_{k>=1} A385429(k)/p^k) = 1.95081275849718099622... .
%t A400871 s[e_] := s[e] = With[{ee = 2*e}, Count[Tuples[Divisors[ee], {2}], _?(Divisible[ee, Total@#] &)]];
%t A400871 f[p_, e_] := s[e]; a[1] = 1; a[n_] := Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[n]; Array[a, 88]
%o A400871 (PARI) s(e) = {my(ee = 2*e, f = factor(ee)); sumdiv(f, d1, sumdiv(f, d2, ee % (d1+d2) == 0));}
%o A400871 a(n) = vecprod(apply(s, factor(n)[, 2]));
%Y A400871 Cf. A049419, A322791, A385429.
%Y A400871 Analogous sequences: A007425 (with all the divisors), A074816 (unitary), A400870 (infinitary).
%K A400871 nonn,mult,easy,new
%O A400871 1,4
%A A400871 _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 08 2026
